Object.assign((window.COI_EXPLAIN = window.COI_EXPLAIN || {}), {
  "52": { "s": "India shall have a President.", "e": "This short article creates the office of the President of India, the head of the Union. The President is the formal head of State and the first citizen, but, as later articles (especially Article 74) make clear, ordinarily acts on the advice of the Council of Ministers headed by the Prime Minister." },
  "53": { "s": "The Union's executive power belongs to the President and is commanded through officers.", "e": "All executive power of the Union is vested in the President, who may exercise it directly or through subordinate officers, in line with the Constitution. The President is also the Supreme Commander of the Defence Forces, though that command must be exercised as regulated by law. Parliament may also give functions to other authorities by law. In practice, the President acts on ministerial advice (Article 74), so real executive power lies with the Union Government." },
  "54": { "s": "The President is elected by MPs and elected members of State and UT assemblies.", "e": "The President is chosen indirectly by an electoral college made up of the elected members of both Houses of Parliament and the elected members of the Legislative Assemblies of the States. Nominated members do not vote. The Seventieth Amendment (1992) added an Explanation so that the elected members of the assemblies of the National Capital Territory of Delhi and the Union territory of Puducherry also vote." },
  "55": { "s": "Rules for weighting votes in the presidential election, using proportional representation.", "e": "Each MLA's vote carries a value based on the population of the State divided by the number of elected MLAs, so that States are represented uniformly by population. The total value of all MLA votes is then divided equally among all elected MPs, giving parity between the States and the Union. Voting is by proportional representation through the single transferable vote, and by secret ballot. Population here means the 1971 census figures until the first census after 2026 is published, a freeze introduced by the Forty-second Amendment and extended by the Eighty-fourth Amendment (2001)." },
  "56": { "s": "The President serves five years, can resign, and stays until a successor takes over.", "e": "The President holds office for five years from the date of entering office. The President may resign by writing to the Vice-President, and may be removed by impeachment under Article 61. Even after the term ends, the President continues in office until the successor takes charge, so the office is never left empty." },
  "57": { "s": "A President can be re-elected.", "e": "A person who holds or has held the office of President is eligible for re-election, subject to the other provisions of the Constitution. There is no constitutional limit on the number of terms; Dr Rajendra Prasad is the only President so far to have served two terms." },
  "58": { "s": "Qualifications to be President: citizen, age 35, eligible for Lok Sabha, no office of profit.", "e": "To be elected President a person must be a citizen of India, at least 35 years old, and qualified to be a member of the Lok Sabha. A person holding an office of profit under the Union, a State, or any local or other authority controlled by them is not eligible. However, the President, Vice-President, a Governor, or a Union or State Minister is not treated as holding an office of profit for this purpose." },
  "59": { "s": "The President cannot be a legislator or hold another office; pay cannot be cut mid-term.", "e": "The President cannot be a member of Parliament or a State Legislature; if a legislator is elected President, the seat is vacated on taking office. The President must not hold any other office of profit. The President gets an official residence free of rent and the emoluments, allowances and privileges fixed by Parliament by law, and these cannot be reduced during the term of office." },
  "60": { "s": "The President takes an oath to preserve, protect and defend the Constitution.", "e": "Before taking office, the President takes an oath or affirmation before the Chief Justice of India (or, in the Chief Justice's absence, the senior-most available Supreme Court judge). The President swears to faithfully execute the office, to preserve, protect and defend the Constitution and the law, and to devote himself or herself to the service and well-being of the people of India." },
  "61": { "s": "How the President can be impeached for violating the Constitution.", "e": "The only ground for impeaching the President is violation of the Constitution. A charge can be started in either House by a resolution moved after at least 14 days' written notice signed by at least one-fourth of that House's total members, and it must be passed by at least two-thirds of the House's total membership. The other House then investigates the charge, and the President has the right to appear and be represented. If that House also declares the charge sustained by a two-thirds majority of its total membership, the President is removed from office from the date the resolution is passed. No President has ever been impeached." },
  "62": { "s": "Timing of presidential elections and the term of a person elected to fill a vacancy.", "e": "The election to fill a vacancy caused by the expiry of the President's term must be completed before the term ends. A vacancy caused by death, resignation, removal or otherwise must be filled by an election held as soon as possible and in any case within six months. A person elected to fill such a vacancy gets a full five-year term, not just the remainder of the predecessor's term." },
  "63": { "s": "India shall have a Vice-President.", "e": "This article creates the office of the Vice-President of India, the second-highest constitutional office. The Vice-President's main regular role is to chair the Rajya Sabha (Article 64) and to act as President when that office is vacant (Article 65)." },
  "64": { "s": "The Vice-President is the ex officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha.", "e": "The Vice-President automatically serves as Chairman of the Council of States (Rajya Sabha) and may not hold any other office of profit. While acting as President or discharging the President's functions under Article 65, the Vice-President does not preside over the Rajya Sabha and does not draw the Chairman's salary." },
  "65": { "s": "The Vice-President acts as President during a vacancy or the President's absence.", "e": "If the President's office falls vacant through death, resignation, removal or otherwise, the Vice-President acts as President until a new President takes office. Similarly, if the President is unable to discharge functions because of absence, illness or any other cause, the Vice-President discharges them until the President resumes duty. While so acting, the Vice-President has all the powers and immunities of the President and is paid the President's emoluments." },
  "66": { "s": "The Vice-President is elected by all members of both Houses of Parliament.", "e": "The Vice-President is elected by an electoral college of all members of both Houses of Parliament, including nominated members, by proportional representation through the single transferable vote and secret ballot. State legislators do not take part. The Vice-President cannot be a member of any legislature and must be a citizen, at least 35 years old, qualified for election to the Rajya Sabha, and not holding an office of profit. The Eleventh Amendment (1961) replaced the original requirement of a joint meeting of both Houses with a simple electoral college." },
  "67": { "s": "The Vice-President serves five years and can be removed by a Rajya Sabha resolution.", "e": "The Vice-President holds office for five years and may resign by writing to the President. The Vice-President can be removed by a resolution of the Rajya Sabha passed by a majority of all its then members and agreed to by the Lok Sabha, after at least 14 days' notice of the intention to move it. Unlike the President, no specific ground is laid down and no formal impeachment process is needed. The Vice-President continues in office after the term ends until a successor takes over." },
  "68": { "s": "Timing of Vice-Presidential elections and the term of a person filling a vacancy.", "e": "The election to fill a vacancy caused by the end of the Vice-President's term must be completed before the term expires. A vacancy caused by death, resignation, removal or otherwise must be filled as soon as possible after it occurs, with no fixed deadline. The person elected gets a full five-year term." },
  "69": { "s": "The Vice-President takes an oath of allegiance to the Constitution.", "e": "Before entering office, the Vice-President takes an oath or affirmation before the President, or a person appointed by the President, to bear true faith and allegiance to the Constitution and to faithfully discharge the duties of the office." },
  "70": { "s": "Parliament may provide for the President's functions in other unforeseen situations.", "e": "Parliament can make law for how the President's functions are to be discharged in contingencies not covered by the Constitution, such as when both the President's and Vice-President's offices are vacant. Under a law made for this purpose, the Chief Justice of India (or, failing that, the senior-most available Supreme Court judge) acts as President in such a situation." },
  "71": { "s": "Disputes about presidential and vice-presidential elections are decided by the Supreme Court.", "e": "All doubts and disputes about the election of a President or Vice-President are decided only by the Supreme Court, whose decision is final. If an election is declared void, acts already done by that person in office remain valid. Parliament may regulate other matters about these elections by law, and an election cannot be challenged merely because there were vacancies in the electoral college. The Thirty-ninth Amendment (1975) had taken such disputes away from the courts, but the Forty-fourth Amendment (1978) restored Supreme Court jurisdiction." },
  "72": { "s": "The President can pardon, reduce or suspend sentences in certain cases.", "e": "The President can grant pardons, reprieves, respites or remissions of punishment, or suspend, remit or commute sentences. This applies to sentences by court martial, offences against laws on matters within the Union's executive power, and all death sentences. It does not affect the pardoning powers of military officers under law or of Governors under Article 161. The President exercises this power on the advice of the Union Council of Ministers, not personally. The Supreme Court has held that the power is not a private act of grace but a constitutional duty, and that its exercise can be reviewed by courts on limited grounds, for example if it is arbitrary, based on irrelevant considerations, made with mala fides, or without applying the mind. Delays in deciding mercy petitions have also been treated by the Court as a ground to commute death sentences." },
  "73": { "s": "The Union's executive power extends to everything Parliament can legislate on.", "e": "The executive power of the Union covers matters on which Parliament can make laws, and rights and powers exercisable by the Government of India under treaties and agreements. It does not, by default, extend in any State to matters on which only the State Legislature can make laws, unless the Constitution or a parliamentary law expressly provides. The Supreme Court has read this to mean the Government can act on such subjects even where no law yet exists, as long as it does not violate any law or right." },
  "74": { "s": "A Council of Ministers led by the Prime Minister advises the President, who must follow it.", "e": "This is the heart of India's parliamentary system: there is a Council of Ministers, headed by the Prime Minister, to aid and advise the President. The Forty-second Amendment (1976) made it explicit that the President shall act in accordance with that advice. The Forty-fourth Amendment (1978) added that the President may ask the Council to reconsider its advice once, but must then act on the advice given after reconsideration. Clause (2) bars courts from inquiring into whether, or what, advice was given to the President. Even before these amendments, the Supreme Court (for example in Shamsher Singh v. State of Punjab, 1974) had held that the President is a constitutional head who acts on ministerial advice, except in a few narrow situations. A small area of discretion remains in practice, such as choosing a Prime Minister when no party has a clear majority." },
  "75": { "s": "Appointment, size, tenure and collective responsibility of Union Ministers.", "e": "The President appoints the Prime Minister, and appoints other Ministers on the Prime Minister's advice. The Ninety-first Amendment (2003) capped the Council of Ministers at 15% of the Lok Sabha's total strength and barred an MP disqualified for defection under the Tenth Schedule from becoming a Minister until the end of their term or re-election. Ministers hold office during the President's pleasure, which in practice means as long as they have the Prime Minister's confidence. The Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha, so a government that loses the House's confidence must resign. Ministers take oaths of office and secrecy (Third Schedule). A non-member can be a Minister, but must become a member of either House within six consecutive months or cease to be a Minister. Ministers' salaries and allowances are set by Parliament by law." },
  "76": { "s": "The Attorney-General is the Union Government's chief law officer.", "e": "The President appoints as Attorney-General for India a person qualified to be a Supreme Court judge. The Attorney-General advises the Government of India on legal matters, performs legal duties assigned by the President, and has the right of audience in all courts in India. The Attorney-General holds office during the President's pleasure and receives remuneration fixed by the President." },
  "77": { "s": "All Union executive action is taken in the President's name under Rules of Business.", "e": "All executive action of the Government of India is expressed to be taken in the President's name, and orders so authenticated cannot be challenged on the ground that the President did not personally make them. The President makes rules for the convenient transaction of government business and its allocation among Ministers, known as the Government of India (Allocation and Transaction of Business) Rules." },
  "78": { "s": "The Prime Minister must keep the President informed about government decisions and plans.", "e": "The Prime Minister must communicate to the President all Cabinet decisions on administration and proposals for legislation, and provide information the President asks for on these matters. If the President requires, the Prime Minister must place before the Council of Ministers any matter decided by a single Minister but not considered by the Council. This is the President's main channel to stay informed and to advise or warn the government." },
  "79": { "s": "Parliament consists of the President, the Rajya Sabha and the Lok Sabha.", "e": "Parliament of the Union is made up of three parts: the President and two Houses, the Council of States (Rajya Sabha) and the House of the People (Lok Sabha). Although the President does not sit in either House, the President is an integral part of Parliament: Bills become law only with presidential assent, and the President summons and prorogues the Houses and can dissolve the Lok Sabha. This reflects India's parliamentary model, in which the executive head is part of the legislature." },
  "80": { "s": "The Rajya Sabha has up to 250 members: 12 nominated and up to 238 from States and UTs.", "e": "The Council of States has 12 members nominated by the President for special knowledge or experience in literature, science, art and social service, and not more than 238 representatives of the States and Union territories. Seats are allotted under the Fourth Schedule, roughly by population. State representatives are elected by the elected members of the State Legislative Assembly using proportional representation by the single transferable vote. Union territory representatives are chosen as Parliament decides by law. The Seventh Amendment (1956) added representation for Union territories." },
  "81": { "s": "The Lok Sabha has up to 530 State members and 20 UT members, allocated by population.", "e": "The House of the People has not more than 530 members directly elected from territorial constituencies in the States and not more than 20 representing the Union territories. Seats are allocated to States so that the ratio of seats to population is as nearly as possible the same for all States (except small States of six million or less), and each State is divided into constituencies of roughly equal population. The Forty-second Amendment (1976) froze the allocation of seats among States at the 1971 census, and the Eighty-fourth Amendment (2001) extended the freeze until the first census after 2026, so that States that controlled population growth are not penalised. Constituency boundaries within States are, under the Eighty-seventh Amendment (2003), based on the 2001 census." },
  "82": { "s": "Lok Sabha seats and constituencies are readjusted after each census, with a freeze until after 2026.", "e": "After every census, the allocation of Lok Sabha seats to States and the drawing of constituency boundaries are to be readjusted by an authority set up by Parliament (the Delimitation Commission). A readjustment does not affect the sitting House and takes effect from a date the President orders. Under the Forty-second, Eighty-fourth and Eighty-seventh Amendments, there is no need to change the number of seats per State (based on the 1971 census) or the constituency boundaries (based on the 2001 census) until figures from the first census after 2026 are published." },
  "83": { "s": "The Rajya Sabha is permanent; the Lok Sabha lasts five years unless dissolved earlier.", "e": "The Rajya Sabha is never dissolved; about one-third of its members retire every two years, so each member serves six years. The Lok Sabha lasts five years from its first meeting unless dissolved sooner, and expiry of that period automatically dissolves it. During a national Emergency, Parliament can extend the Lok Sabha's life by law by up to one year at a time, but not beyond six months after the Emergency ends. The Forty-second Amendment (1976) had raised the term to six years; the Forty-fourth Amendment (1978) restored five years." },
  "84": { "s": "Qualifications to be an MP: citizen, oath, and minimum age of 30 (Rajya Sabha) or 25 (Lok Sabha).", "e": "To be a member of Parliament, a person must be a citizen of India and must take an oath or affirmation, before a person authorised by the Election Commission, in the form in the Third Schedule. The minimum age is 30 for the Rajya Sabha and 25 for the Lok Sabha. Parliament may prescribe other qualifications by law, as it has done in the Representation of the People Act, 1951. The oath requirement, which includes upholding the sovereignty and integrity of India, was added by the Sixteenth Amendment (1963)." },
  "85": { "s": "The President summons and prorogues Parliament and can dissolve the Lok Sabha.", "e": "The President must summon each House to meet from time to time, and no more than six months may pass between the last sitting of one session and the first sitting of the next. This means Parliament must meet at least twice a year; in practice it usually has three sessions (Budget, Monsoon and Winter). The President may also prorogue (formally end a session of) either House and may dissolve the Lok Sabha, leading to fresh elections. All these powers are exercised on the advice of the Council of Ministers. The present form of this article was substituted by the Constitution (First Amendment) Act, 1951." },
  "86": { "s": "The President may address Parliament and send messages to either House.", "e": "The President may address either House or both Houses together and may require members to attend. The President may also send messages to either House about a pending Bill or anything else, and the House must consider the matter promptly. In practice such messages are rare, and they are sent on ministerial advice." },
  "87": { "s": "The President addresses both Houses after each general election and at the start of each year.", "e": "At the start of the first session after each Lok Sabha general election and at the start of the first session of each year, the President addresses both Houses together and explains why Parliament has been summoned. This address, written by the government, sets out its policy agenda, and each House then debates a 'Motion of Thanks' on it. The First Amendment (1951) changed the requirement from every session to the first session of each year." },
  "88": { "s": "Ministers and the Attorney-General may speak in either House but cannot vote there.", "e": "Every Minister and the Attorney-General may speak in and take part in the proceedings of either House, any joint sitting, and any parliamentary committee of which they are named members. They cannot vote, however, except in the House of which they are actually members. This allows, for example, a Minister from the Rajya Sabha to answer questions or pilot a Bill in the Lok Sabha." },
  "89": { "s": "The Vice-President chairs the Rajya Sabha; the House elects its own Deputy Chairman.", "e": "The Vice-President is the Chairman of the Rajya Sabha. The House also chooses one of its members as Deputy Chairman and elects a new one whenever the office falls vacant." },
  "90": { "s": "How the Deputy Chairman of the Rajya Sabha leaves office or is removed.", "e": "The Deputy Chairman vacates office on ceasing to be a member of the Rajya Sabha, may resign by writing to the Chairman, and may be removed by a resolution passed by a majority of all the then members of the House. At least 14 days' notice must be given of the intention to move such a resolution." },
  "91": { "s": "The Deputy Chairman or another member acts for the Chairman when needed.", "e": "When the Chairman's office is vacant or the Vice-President is acting as President, the Deputy Chairman performs the Chairman's duties; if that office is also vacant, a member appointed by the President does so. During the Chairman's absence from a sitting, the Deputy Chairman presides, or, if absent too, a person decided by the House's rules (a panel of vice-chairmen) or by the House itself." },
  "92": { "s": "The Chairman or Deputy Chairman cannot preside while a resolution to remove them is considered.", "e": "While a resolution to remove the Vice-President (as Chairman) or the Deputy Chairman is being considered, that person cannot preside, though they may still attend. The Chairman may speak and take part in the proceedings but cannot vote at all on the resolution or on any other matter during that time. This protects the fairness of the process." },
  "93": { "s": "The Lok Sabha elects its own Speaker and Deputy Speaker.", "e": "The House of the People chooses two of its members as Speaker and Deputy Speaker as soon as possible, and fills any vacancy by electing another member. The Speaker presides over the Lok Sabha, maintains order and decides many procedural questions; the Deputy Speaker stands in when needed." },
  "94": { "s": "How the Speaker and Deputy Speaker leave office or are removed.", "e": "The Speaker or Deputy Speaker vacates office on ceasing to be a member of the Lok Sabha, may resign in writing (the Speaker to the Deputy Speaker and vice versa), and may be removed by a resolution passed by a majority of all the then members of the House after at least 14 days' notice. When the Lok Sabha is dissolved, the Speaker does not vacate office until just before the first meeting of the new House, so there is always a Speaker." },
  "95": { "s": "The Deputy Speaker or another member acts for the Speaker when needed.", "e": "If the Speaker's office is vacant, the Deputy Speaker performs the Speaker's duties; if that office is also vacant, a member appointed by the President does so. During the Speaker's absence from a sitting, the Deputy Speaker presides, or, if also absent, a person decided under the House's rules (a panel of chairpersons) or by the House." },
  "96": { "s": "The Speaker or Deputy Speaker cannot preside while a resolution to remove them is considered.", "e": "While a resolution to remove the Speaker or Deputy Speaker is under consideration, that person cannot preside, although they may attend. The Speaker may speak and take part in the proceedings and, unlike the Rajya Sabha Chairman, may vote in the first instance on the resolution or other matters, but not in case of a tie." },
  "97": { "s": "Salaries of the presiding officers of both Houses are fixed by Parliament by law.", "e": "The Chairman and Deputy Chairman of the Rajya Sabha and the Speaker and Deputy Speaker of the Lok Sabha receive salaries and allowances fixed by Parliament by law, and until then as set out in the Second Schedule. Their salaries are charged on the Consolidated Fund of India, so they are not subject to an annual vote, which supports their independence." },
  "98": { "s": "Each House of Parliament has its own separate secretarial staff.", "e": "Each House of Parliament has its own secretarial staff, though some posts may be common to both. Parliament may by law regulate the recruitment and service conditions of this staff; until then, the President may make rules after consulting the Speaker or the Chairman. This keeps parliamentary staff independent of the government's civil service." },
  "99": { "s": "Every MP must take an oath before taking a seat.", "e": "Before taking a seat, every member of either House must make and subscribe an oath or affirmation before the President or a person appointed by the President, in the form given in the Third Schedule. The oath includes bearing true faith and allegiance to the Constitution and upholding the sovereignty and integrity of India." },
  "100": { "s": "Decisions are by majority vote; presiding officer has a casting vote; quorum is one-tenth.", "e": "Unless the Constitution says otherwise (for example, special majorities for constitutional amendments), questions in either House or a joint sitting are decided by a majority of members present and voting. The presiding officer does not vote at first but has a casting vote if there is a tie. A House can act despite vacancies, and proceedings are not invalid merely because someone not entitled to sit took part. Until Parliament provides otherwise, the quorum is one-tenth of the House's total membership. The Forty-second Amendment's attempt to omit the quorum clauses never came into force and was itself removed by the Forty-fourth Amendment." },
  "101": { "s": "When an MP's seat becomes vacant, including resignations and long absence.", "e": "No one can be a member of both Houses of Parliament, or of Parliament and a State Legislature at the same time; the law sets out how one seat is given up. A seat becomes vacant if the member becomes disqualified under Article 102 (including for defection under the Tenth Schedule), or resigns in writing and the resignation is accepted. Under the Thirty-third Amendment (1974), the Speaker or Chairman may refuse a resignation if satisfied that it is not voluntary or genuine. A House may also declare a seat vacant if the member is absent from all meetings for 60 days without permission." },
  "102": { "s": "Grounds on which a person is disqualified from being an MP, including defection.", "e": "A person is disqualified from being chosen as, or remaining, an MP if they hold an office of profit under the Union or a State government (unless Parliament has exempted that office by law), are declared of unsound mind by a court, are an undischarged insolvent, are not an Indian citizen or have acquired foreign citizenship or allegiance, or are disqualified under a law made by Parliament (such as the Representation of the People Act, 1951). Being a Minister is not an office of profit. The Fifty-second Amendment (1985) added clause (2), making defection under the Tenth Schedule a ground for disqualification." },
  "103": { "s": "The President decides questions of MP disqualification after taking the Election Commission's opinion.", "e": "If a question arises whether a sitting MP has become disqualified under Article 102(1), it is referred to the President, whose decision is final. Before deciding, the President must obtain the opinion of the Election Commission and act according to it. This procedure does not cover defection; disqualification under the Tenth Schedule is decided by the Speaker or Chairman." },
  "104": { "s": "Penalty for sitting or voting in Parliament without oath or while disqualified.", "e": "A person who sits or votes as a member without having taken the oath under Article 99, or knowing they are not qualified or are disqualified, or are barred by law, is liable to a penalty of 500 rupees for each day, recoverable as a debt due to the Union." },
  "105": { "s": "Freedom of speech and other privileges of Parliament, its members and committees.", "e": "There is freedom of speech in Parliament, subject to the Constitution and the rules of procedure. No MP can be taken to court for anything said or any vote given in Parliament or its committees, and no one is liable for publishing reports or proceedings under the authority of a House. Other powers and privileges are to be defined by Parliament by law; since no such law has been made, they remain as they were before the Forty-fourth Amendment (1978), which removed the earlier reference to the British House of Commons while keeping the same position. The same protections extend to non-members entitled to speak in Parliament, such as the Attorney-General and Ministers. In P.V. Narasimha Rao (1998), the Supreme Court held that MPs who took bribes to vote were immune, but a seven-judge bench overruled this in Sita Soren v. Union of India (2024), holding that bribery is not protected by parliamentary privilege. Courts may examine whether a claimed privilege exists, and Parliament's power to punish for contempt has been upheld." },
  "106": { "s": "MPs' salaries and allowances are fixed by Parliament by law.", "e": "Members of either House receive salaries and allowances as Parliament may decide by law. Until such a law was made, the allowances were those that applied to members of the Constituent Assembly. Parliament now regulates these under the Salary, Allowances and Pension of Members of Parliament Act, 1954." },
  "107": { "s": "How ordinary Bills are introduced and passed, and when they lapse.", "e": "Apart from Money Bills and other financial Bills (which must start in the Lok Sabha), a Bill can be introduced in either House. A Bill is passed only when both Houses agree to it, with or without amendments. Pending Bills do not lapse when Parliament is prorogued. A Bill pending in the Rajya Sabha that the Lok Sabha has not passed survives the Lok Sabha's dissolution, but a Bill pending in the Lok Sabha, or passed by the Lok Sabha and pending in the Rajya Sabha, lapses when the Lok Sabha is dissolved." },
  "108": { "s": "A joint sitting of both Houses resolves deadlocks over ordinary Bills.", "e": "If one House rejects a Bill passed by the other, the Houses finally disagree on amendments, or more than six months pass without the other House passing it, the President may summon a joint sitting of both Houses. The Bill is then passed by a majority of the total members of both Houses present and voting, so the larger Lok Sabha usually prevails. A joint sitting cannot be called for a Money Bill, and in practice it is not available for constitutional amendments, which follow Article 368. A joint sitting can still be held after the Lok Sabha is dissolved if it had been notified before. Joint sittings have been held only three times, for example on the Prevention of Terrorism Bill in 2002." },
  "109": { "s": "Money Bills start in the Lok Sabha; the Rajya Sabha can only recommend changes within 14 days.", "e": "A Money Bill can only be introduced in the Lok Sabha. After the Lok Sabha passes it, the Rajya Sabha must return it within 14 days, with or without recommendations. The Lok Sabha may accept or reject any of these recommendations, and if the Rajya Sabha does not return the Bill within 14 days, it is deemed passed by both Houses. This gives the directly elected House the final say on money matters." },
  "110": { "s": "Defines a Money Bill and makes the Speaker's certification decisive.", "e": "A Bill is a Money Bill only if it contains only provisions on matters such as imposing, abolishing or changing taxes, government borrowing or guarantees, the custody of and payments into or out of the Consolidated Fund or Contingency Fund of India, appropriation of money from the Consolidated Fund, charged expenditure, receipts and audit of public accounts, or matters incidental to these. A Bill is not a Money Bill merely because it provides for fines, fees for services, or local taxation. If there is a doubt, the Speaker's decision is final, and the Speaker's certificate is endorsed on the Bill. The word 'only' is significant: a Bill with other substantive provisions should not be certified as a Money Bill. In the Aadhaar case (K.S. Puttaswamy, 2018) the majority upheld certification of the Aadhaar Act as a Money Bill, with a strong dissent, and in Rojer Mathew v. South Indian Bank (2019) the question of the scope of Article 110 and judicial review of the Speaker's certificate was referred to a larger bench." },
  "111": { "s": "The President may assent to, withhold assent from, or return a Bill once for reconsideration.", "e": "Once both Houses pass a Bill, it is presented to the President, who may give assent or withhold it. For any Bill other than a Money Bill, the President may return it with a message asking the Houses to reconsider it or any of its provisions. If the Houses pass it again, with or without amendments, the President cannot withhold assent. No time limit is fixed for the President's decision." },
  "112": { "s": "The President must lay the annual Budget (annual financial statement) before Parliament.", "e": "Every financial year, the President causes an 'annual financial statement', commonly called the Budget, to be laid before both Houses, showing estimated receipts and expenditure of the Government of India. It must show separately expenditure 'charged' on the Consolidated Fund (which is not put to vote) and other expenditure, and distinguish revenue spending from other spending. Charged expenditure includes the President's emoluments, salaries of the presiding officers of Parliament, salaries and pensions of Supreme Court judges and the Comptroller and Auditor-General, pensions of High Court judges, debt charges, and sums needed to satisfy court judgments. The word 'Budget' itself does not appear in the Constitution." },
  "113": { "s": "Charged expenditure is debated but not voted; other spending is voted as demands for grants.", "e": "Estimates of expenditure charged on the Consolidated Fund can be discussed in Parliament but are not put to vote. Other estimates are presented to the Lok Sabha as demands for grants, which it may approve, reject or reduce. No demand for a grant can be made except on the recommendation of the President, which means only the government can propose spending." },
  "114": { "s": "After grants are voted, an Appropriation Bill authorises withdrawals from the Consolidated Fund.", "e": "After the Lok Sabha votes the grants, an Appropriation Bill is introduced to withdraw from the Consolidated Fund the money needed for those grants and for charged expenditure. No amendment may be proposed that changes the amount or purpose of a grant or the amount of charged expenditure. No money can be withdrawn from the Consolidated Fund except under an appropriation made by law, which secures Parliament's control over public spending." },
  "115": { "s": "Parliament's approval for supplementary, additional or excess spending.", "e": "If the sums authorised for a year prove insufficient, a new service is needed, or more money than was granted has actually been spent, the President must lay before Parliament a statement of the further expenditure or a demand for the excess. These are then dealt with in the same way as the annual budget, through demands and an Appropriation Bill." },
  "116": { "s": "Votes on account, votes of credit and exceptional grants for interim or unusual spending.", "e": "The Lok Sabha can make a grant in advance for part of the year (a 'vote on account') while the full budget is still being passed, which is commonly done in election years. It can also make a 'vote of credit' for an unexpected demand whose scope or details cannot be stated in the usual way, and an 'exceptional grant' that is not part of the current service of any financial year. Parliament must still pass a law to withdraw the money." },
  "117": { "s": "Special rules for financial Bills that are not pure Money Bills.", "e": "A Bill that deals with any of the Money Bill subjects in Article 110, but not only those subjects, cannot be introduced or amendments to it moved without the President's recommendation, and must start in the Lok Sabha. Apart from that, the Rajya Sabha has full powers over it. A Bill that would involve spending from the Consolidated Fund cannot be passed by either House unless the President has recommended its consideration. An amendment reducing or abolishing a tax does not need a recommendation." },
  "118": { "s": "Each House makes its own rules of procedure; joint sitting rules are set by the Speaker.", "e": "Each House of Parliament can make rules to regulate its procedure and conduct of business, subject to the Constitution. Until then, rules that applied to the pre-independence Legislature continue with modifications by the presiding officer. The President, after consulting the Chairman and Speaker, makes rules for joint sittings and communication between the Houses, and the Speaker presides at a joint sitting." },
  "119": { "s": "Parliament may regulate financial business by law.", "e": "For timely completion of financial business, Parliament may regulate by law the procedure and conduct of business in each House relating to financial matters and the Consolidated Fund. Such a law prevails over the House's own rules where they conflict." },
  "120": { "s": "Parliament works in Hindi or English; members may speak their mother tongue with permission.", "e": "Business in Parliament is transacted in Hindi or English, notwithstanding Part XVII on official language. The presiding officer may allow a member who cannot adequately express themselves in Hindi or English to speak in their mother tongue. The provision for English was meant to lapse after 15 years unless Parliament decided otherwise, but its use has continued." },
  "121": { "s": "Parliament cannot discuss a judge's conduct except on a motion for removal.", "e": "Parliament may not discuss the conduct of any Supreme Court or High Court judge in the discharge of duties, except on a motion to present an address to the President for that judge's removal. This protects judicial independence by keeping judges' work out of routine political debate." },
  "122": { "s": "Courts cannot question proceedings of Parliament for irregular procedure.", "e": "The validity of any proceedings in Parliament cannot be questioned in court on the ground of an alleged irregularity of procedure. Officers and members who regulate procedure, business or order in Parliament are not subject to courts' jurisdiction in the exercise of those powers. The Supreme Court (for example in Raja Ram Pal, 2007) has held that this bar covers mere procedural irregularity, but courts can still examine proceedings that are illegal or unconstitutional." },
  "123": { "s": "The President can issue ordinances with force of law when Parliament is not in session.", "e": "When both Houses are not in session, the President may issue an Ordinance if satisfied that circumstances require immediate action. An Ordinance has the same force as an Act of Parliament but must be laid before both Houses when they reassemble; it lapses six weeks after reassembly unless replaced by an Act, or earlier if both Houses pass resolutions disapproving it. The President may withdraw it at any time, and it is void to the extent it does something Parliament could not legally enact. The President acts on the advice of the Council of Ministers. The Thirty-eighth Amendment (1975) had made the President's satisfaction immune from court review, but the Forty-fourth Amendment (1978) removed that clause. The Supreme Court has held that the satisfaction can be reviewed, and, in cases on State ordinances (D.C. Wadhwa, 1987, and Krishna Kumar Singh, 2017), that repeatedly re-issuing ordinances without placing them before the legislature is a fraud on the Constitution." },
  "124": { "s": "Establishes the Supreme Court; appointment, tenure, qualifications and removal of judges.", "e": "There is a Supreme Court with a Chief Justice and other judges; the Constitution originally allowed seven others, and Parliament has raised this by law (since a 2026 amendment, 37 other judges, making 38 in all). Judges are appointed by the President and serve until 65; they can resign or be removed. A candidate must be a citizen and have been a High Court judge for five years, an advocate of a High Court for ten years, or a distinguished jurist in the President's opinion. A judge can be removed only for proved misbehaviour or incapacity, by an order of the President after each House passes an address by a majority of its total membership and two-thirds of those present and voting, in the same session; the procedure is set out in the Judges (Inquiry) Act, 1968. On appointments, the original text required consultation with judges. In the Second Judges case (1993) and the Third Judges case (1998 opinion), the Supreme Court read 'consultation' to give primacy to the Chief Justice and senior judges, creating the collegium system. The Ninety-ninth Amendment (2014) replaced this with the National Judicial Appointments Commission, but the Supreme Court struck that amendment down in 2015 (Supreme Court Advocates-on-Record Association v. Union of India), so the collegium system continues. The Fifteenth Amendment (1963) added clause (2A) on determining a judge's age." },
  "124A": { "s": "National Judicial Appointments Commission (inserted 2014; struck down by the Supreme Court in 2015).", "e": "The Ninety-ninth Amendment (2014) inserted this article to create a six-member National Judicial Appointments Commission: the Chief Justice of India, the two next senior Supreme Court judges, the Union Law Minister, and two eminent persons chosen by the Prime Minister, Chief Justice and Leader of Opposition. In October 2015 the Supreme Court struck down the amendment as violating judicial independence, part of the basic structure, so the Commission never functions and the collegium system continues. The text still appears in official versions with a note recording the judgment." },
  "124B": { "s": "Functions of the NJAC (struck down by the Supreme Court in 2015).", "e": "This article, inserted by the Ninety-ninth Amendment (2014), would have made the NJAC responsible for recommending appointments of the Chief Justice of India and Supreme Court and High Court judges, recommending transfers of High Court judges, and ensuring that persons recommended have ability and integrity. The Supreme Court struck the amendment down in 2015, so this article is not operative." },
  "124C": { "s": "Parliament's power to regulate NJAC procedure (struck down by the Supreme Court in 2015).", "e": "Inserted by the Ninety-ninth Amendment (2014), this article let Parliament regulate the procedure for appointing judges and empower the NJAC to frame its own regulations; Parliament passed the NJAC Act, 2014 under it. The Supreme Court struck down both the amendment and the Act in 2015, so this article has no effect." },
  "125": { "s": "Supreme Court judges' salaries are fixed by law and cannot be cut after appointment.", "e": "Supreme Court judges are paid salaries determined by Parliament by law, and until then as specified in the Second Schedule. Their privileges, allowances, leave and pension rights are also fixed by law but cannot be varied to their disadvantage after appointment. The Fifty-fourth Amendment (1986) allowed salaries to be revised by ordinary law rather than by amending the Constitution. This protection supports judicial independence." },
  "126": { "s": "The President may appoint an acting Chief Justice of India when needed.", "e": "If the office of Chief Justice of India is vacant, or the Chief Justice is absent or unable to perform duties, the President appoints one of the other Supreme Court judges to perform those duties. By convention, this is the senior-most judge." },
  "127": { "s": "High Court judges may be appointed as ad hoc Supreme Court judges when there is no quorum.", "e": "If there are not enough Supreme Court judges to form a quorum, a qualified High Court judge can be asked to sit as an ad hoc judge of the Supreme Court, after consulting that High Court's Chief Justice and with the President's previous consent. The judge must give these duties priority and has full powers of a Supreme Court judge while sitting. The Ninety-ninth Amendment (2014) gave the request power to the NJAC, but since that amendment was struck down in 2015, the original text, under which the Chief Justice of India makes the request, applies." },
  "128": { "s": "Retired judges may be requested to sit and act as Supreme Court judges.", "e": "With the President's previous consent, a retired judge of the Supreme Court or Federal Court, or a retired High Court judge qualified for Supreme Court appointment, may be requested to sit and act as a Supreme Court judge, but only with their consent. They have all the powers of a Supreme Court judge while sitting, but are not otherwise deemed to be one. The Fifteenth Amendment (1963) added retired High Court judges. The Ninety-ninth Amendment (2014) gave the request power to the NJAC, but since that amendment was struck down in 2015, the Chief Justice of India makes the request as before." },
  "129": { "s": "The Supreme Court is a court of record with power to punish for contempt.", "e": "As a court of record, the Supreme Court's judgments and proceedings are preserved as permanent records with evidentiary value. The article also confirms that the Court has all the powers of such a court, including the power to punish for contempt of itself. The Supreme Court has held that this power is inherent and cannot be taken away by ordinary law, though the Contempt of Courts Act, 1971 guides its exercise." },
  "130": { "s": "The Supreme Court sits in Delhi or anywhere else the Chief Justice decides.", "e": "The Supreme Court sits in Delhi, or in such other place or places as the Chief Justice of India may, with the President's approval, decide from time to time. Proposals for regional benches have been discussed, but the Court continues to sit in Delhi." },
  "131": { "s": "The Supreme Court alone decides legal disputes between the Union and States, or between States.", "e": "The Supreme Court has exclusive original jurisdiction, meaning cases start there, in disputes between the Government of India and one or more States, between the Union and some States on one side and other States on the other, or between two or more States, if the dispute involves a question of law or fact on which a legal right depends. It does not cover disputes arising from treaties, agreements, sanads or similar instruments made before the Constitution that are still in force, or from any such instrument that itself excludes this jurisdiction. Private parties cannot bring cases under this article. Some subjects, such as inter-State river water disputes, are excluded by Parliament under Article 262. The Seventh Amendment (1956) substituted the proviso." },
  "131A": { "s": "Omitted — formerly gave the Supreme Court exclusive power to decide validity of Central laws.", "e": "Inserted by the Forty-second Amendment (1976), this article gave the Supreme Court exclusive jurisdiction over questions about the constitutional validity of Central laws, taking that power away from High Courts. The Forty-third Amendment (1977) omitted it with effect from 13 April 1978." },
  "132": { "s": "Appeals to the Supreme Court in cases involving a substantial constitutional question.", "e": "An appeal lies to the Supreme Court from any judgment, decree or final order of a High Court, in civil, criminal or other proceedings, if the High Court certifies under Article 134A that the case involves a substantial question of law about the interpretation of the Constitution. In such an appeal, a party can argue that the question was wrongly decided. The Forty-fourth Amendment (1978) linked the certificate to the new Article 134A procedure." },
  "133": { "s": "Civil appeals to the Supreme Court on certified questions of law of general importance.", "e": "An appeal in a civil case lies from a High Court to the Supreme Court if the High Court certifies under Article 134A that the case involves a substantial question of law of general importance that, in its opinion, needs to be decided by the Supreme Court. The appellant can also argue that a constitutional question was wrongly decided. Unless Parliament provides otherwise, there is no appeal from a judgment of a single High Court judge. The Thirtieth Amendment (1972) removed the earlier test based on the money value of the dispute." },
  "134": { "s": "Criminal appeals to the Supreme Court, including in death-sentence cases.", "e": "An appeal lies to the Supreme Court in a criminal case as of right if a High Court has reversed an acquittal and sentenced the accused to death, or has withdrawn a case from a lower court, tried it, and sentenced the accused to death. In other cases, an appeal lies if the High Court certifies under Article 134A that it is a fit case for appeal. Parliament can expand this jurisdiction by law, and has done so for certain cases of life imprisonment or long sentences on reversal of acquittal." },
  "134A": { "s": "How and when a High Court grants a certificate for appeal to the Supreme Court.", "e": "Inserted by the Forty-fourth Amendment (1978), this article requires a High Court that passes a judgment, decree, final order or sentence to decide, on its own or on an oral application made immediately afterwards, whether to grant a certificate for appeal under Article 132, 133 or 134(1)(c). This is meant to save time and make the procedure for appeals clearer." },
  "135": { "s": "The Supreme Court inherits the old Federal Court's jurisdiction where no other article applies.", "e": "Until Parliament provides otherwise, the Supreme Court can exercise any jurisdiction and powers that the Federal Court had under existing law just before the Constitution began, in matters not covered by Articles 133 or 134. This was a transitional provision to ensure no gap in jurisdiction when the Supreme Court replaced the Federal Court in 1950." },
  "136": { "s": "The Supreme Court may grant special leave to appeal from any court or tribunal.", "e": "The Supreme Court may, in its discretion, grant special leave to appeal from any judgment, decree, determination, sentence or order passed by any court or tribunal in India, except military tribunals under laws relating to the armed forces. This is the route of the 'Special Leave Petition' (SLP), which is the most common way cases reach the Supreme Court. It is a wide, residuary power that does not depend on a High Court certificate. The Supreme Court has repeatedly said that it is discretionary and to be used sparingly, in cases of grave injustice or important questions of law, and that it gives no right of appeal. In practice, however, the large number of SLPs has contributed to the Court's heavy caseload." },
  "137": { "s": "The Supreme Court can review its own judgments.", "e": "Subject to any law made by Parliament and rules under Article 145, the Supreme Court can review any judgment or order it has pronounced. Under its rules, review is limited to grounds such as an error apparent on the record and is usually heard by the same judges, often in chambers. The Court has also recognised a further, narrower 'curative petition' remedy after a review is dismissed (Rupa Ashok Hurra, 2002), to prevent gross miscarriage of justice." },
  "138": { "s": "Parliament or agreement with the Union can enlarge the Supreme Court's jurisdiction.", "e": "Parliament may by law give the Supreme Court further jurisdiction and powers on any matter in the Union List. The Supreme Court can also have further jurisdiction on any matter if the Government of India and a State government agree specially and Parliament provides for it by law." },
  "139": { "s": "Parliament may let the Supreme Court issue writs for purposes beyond fundamental rights.", "e": "Under Article 32 the Supreme Court issues writs to enforce fundamental rights. This article allows Parliament by law to empower the Court to issue directions, orders or writs, including habeas corpus, mandamus, prohibition, quo warranto and certiorari, for other purposes as well." },
  "139A": { "s": "The Supreme Court can transfer cases between High Courts or to itself.", "e": "If cases involving the same or substantially the same questions of law are pending before the Supreme Court and one or more High Courts, or before two or more High Courts, and these are substantial questions of general importance, the Supreme Court may withdraw the cases and decide them itself, on its own motion, on application by the Attorney-General, or by a party. It may also transfer any case from one High Court to another in the interest of justice. Inserted by the Forty-second Amendment (1976), clause (1) was rewritten by the Forty-fourth Amendment (1978)." },
  "140": { "s": "Parliament may give the Supreme Court supplementary powers to exercise its jurisdiction.", "e": "Parliament may by law confer on the Supreme Court supplementary powers not inconsistent with the Constitution, as needed to help the Court exercise its jurisdiction more effectively." },
  "141": { "s": "Law declared by the Supreme Court binds all courts in India.", "e": "The law declared by the Supreme Court is binding on all courts in India. This is what gives Supreme Court decisions the force of precedent nationwide: High Courts and lower courts must follow them. What binds is the legal principle (the ratio) behind a decision, not every passing remark, though courts also give considerable weight to the Court's considered observations (obiter). The Supreme Court is not itself bound by its earlier decisions and can overrule them, usually through a larger bench. Smaller benches are bound by decisions of larger benches, and a bench that doubts a coordinate bench's decision should refer the matter to a larger bench." },
  "142": { "s": "The Supreme Court can make any order needed to do 'complete justice' in a case.", "e": "Clause (1) lets the Supreme Court pass any decree or order necessary to do complete justice in any case before it, enforceable throughout India as Parliament or, until then, the President provides. Clause (2) gives it powers to secure attendance of persons, discovery or production of documents, and to investigate or punish contempt of itself, subject to any law made by Parliament. The 'complete justice' power is a wide, residuary power that the Court has used to fill legal gaps and provide remedies, for example in laying down guidelines where there was no legislation, in the Union Carbide (Bhopal) settlement, and in dissolving marriages that had irretrievably broken down. The Court has said this power is meant to supplement, not override, substantive law; in Supreme Court Bar Association v. Union of India (1998) it held that Article 142 cannot be used to ignore express statutory provisions on the subject. Critics argue it gives the Court very broad scope, so its use is closely watched." },
  "143": { "s": "The President may ask the Supreme Court for its opinion on important questions.", "e": "If it appears to the President that a question of law or fact of public importance has arisen or is likely to arise, the President may refer it to the Supreme Court for its opinion, and the Court may, after hearing, report its opinion. For disputes arising from pre-Constitution treaties and agreements excluded from Article 131, the Court must give its opinion. Under Article 145(3), such references are heard by at least five judges. This 'advisory jurisdiction' has been used for questions such as the Kerala Education Bill (1958), Berubari (1960) and judicial appointments (the Third Judges case, 1998). In the general category, the Court is not obliged to answer and has declined in some cases, as with the Ayodhya reference in 1994. An advisory opinion is highly persuasive, but there is debate over whether it is binding like a judgment under Article 141." },
  "144": { "s": "All civil and judicial authorities in India must act in aid of the Supreme Court.", "e": "Every civil and judicial authority in India must act in aid of the Supreme Court. This helps ensure that the Court's orders and decrees are carried out throughout the country." },
  "144A": { "s": "Omitted — formerly required larger benches and special majorities to strike down laws.", "e": "Inserted by the Forty-second Amendment (1976), this article required at least seven judges to decide the constitutional validity of a Central or State law and a two-thirds majority to declare it invalid. The Forty-third Amendment (1977) omitted it with effect from 13 April 1978." },
  "145": { "s": "The Supreme Court makes its own rules; constitutional questions need at least five judges.", "e": "With the President's approval, and subject to parliamentary law, the Supreme Court may make rules on its practice and procedure, including who may practise before it, appeals, enforcement of fundamental rights, transfers under Article 139A, review, costs, bail, stays, and summary dismissal of frivolous appeals. Cases involving a substantial question of law on interpreting the Constitution, and references under Article 143, must be heard by at least five judges (a Constitution Bench). Judgments must be delivered in open court, require the agreement of a majority of the judges hearing the case, and judges who disagree may give dissenting opinions. The Forty-second (1976) and Forty-third (1977) Amendments altered this article, the latter removing the special provisions tied to Articles 131A and 144A." },
  "146": { "s": "The Chief Justice appoints Supreme Court staff; Court expenses are charged on the Consolidated Fund.", "e": "Officers and staff of the Supreme Court are appointed by the Chief Justice of India or a judge or officer he directs, and the President may require consultation with the UPSC for certain posts. Subject to law, service conditions are set by rules made by the Chief Justice of India or an authorised judge or officer, with rules on salaries, allowances, leave and pensions needing the President's approval. The Court's administrative expenses, including salaries and pensions of staff, are charged on the Consolidated Fund of India, so they are not voted on annually, which supports the Court's independence." },
  "147": { "s": "Explains what 'substantial question of law as to the interpretation of this Constitution' includes.", "e": "For this Chapter and Chapter V of Part VI, references to a substantial question of law about interpreting the Constitution include questions about interpreting the Government of India Act, 1935, the Indian Independence Act, 1947, and related orders. This ensured continuity in deciding disputes rooted in pre-Constitution laws." },
  "148": { "s": "The Comptroller and Auditor-General is appointed by the President and removable only like a Supreme Court judge.", "e": "The Comptroller and Auditor-General of India (CAG) is appointed by the President and can be removed only in the same way and on the same grounds as a Supreme Court judge. The CAG's salary and service conditions are fixed by Parliament by law (until then, as in the Second Schedule) and cannot be changed to their disadvantage after appointment. After leaving office, the CAG cannot hold any further office under the Union or any State government. The CAG's salary and the expenses of the CAG's office are charged on the Consolidated Fund of India. These safeguards are meant to make the CAG independent of the government it audits. By statute, the CAG holds office for six years or until age 65, whichever is earlier." },
  "149": { "s": "The CAG audits Union and State accounts as Parliament provides by law.", "e": "The CAG performs duties and exercises powers relating to the accounts of the Union, the States and other authorities or bodies as Parliament prescribes by law, and until then as the Auditor-General did before the Constitution. Parliament has enacted the Comptroller and Auditor-General's (Duties, Powers and Conditions of Service) Act, 1971 for this purpose." },
  "150": { "s": "The President prescribes the form of Union and State accounts on the CAG's advice.", "e": "The accounts of the Union and of the States are kept in the form that the President prescribes on the advice of the Comptroller and Auditor-General. The Forty-fourth Amendment (1978) changed 'after consultation with' to 'on the advice of', strengthening the CAG's role." },
  "151": { "s": "CAG audit reports are placed before Parliament and State Legislatures.", "e": "The CAG's reports on the accounts of the Union are submitted to the President, who has them laid before each House of Parliament. Reports on a State's accounts are submitted to the Governor, who has them laid before the State Legislature. These reports are examined in detail by the Public Accounts Committee, making the CAG a key tool of legislative control over government spending." }
});
